NAME
    search - iterative memory value and pointer search

SYNOPSIS
    search <space> <value> [<width>] [flags]
    search unknown <space> [flags]
    search eq|ne|gt|lt <space> <value> [flags]
    search between <space> <lo> <hi> [flags]
    search refine|eq|ne|gt|lt <value> [flags]
    search between <lo> <hi> [flags]
    search changed|unchanged|increased|decreased [flags]
    search incby|decby <delta> [flags]
    search pointer <space> <target> [--max-offset N] [--base-start A] [--base-end B] [--le|--be] [--signed-offset] [--json]
    search pointer rescan <target> [--max-offset N] [--json]
    search undo [--json]
    search list [<count>] [--dec|--hex] [--json]
    search reset [--json]

DESCRIPTION
    Finds unknown memory addresses (scores, lives, coordinates, floats, or
    pointers) by capturing a memory space snapshot and narrowing candidates
    as gameplay progresses.

    One search session is active per shell with up to 8 undo steps backed
    by compact bitset/sparse storage. Endianness defaults to the target
    space's hardware byte order unless overridden with --le or --be.
    Refuses spaces backed by live hardware registers with read side
    effects (such as CpuBus); use a plain memory space such as WRAM or
    RDRAM.

OPTIONS
    -w <1|2|4>, --width <1|2|4>
        Scalar width in bytes (default 1; 4 when --float is given).

    --signed | --unsigned | --float
        Interpret bytes as signed integer, unsigned integer (default), or
        32-bit IEEE-754 float (requires width 4).

    --le | --be
        Override little-endian or big-endian byte order.

    --dec | --hex
        Parse and display values in decimal or hexadecimal (default hex).

    --aligned | --unaligned
        Step by <width> bytes or by 1 byte. Positional 'search <space>
        <value>' defaults to unaligned; 'search unknown|eq|ne|gt|lt|between'
        defaults to aligned when width > 1 on spaces larger than 64 KiB.

    --json
        Emit structured JSON output.

    unknown <space>
        Capture a full baseline snapshot without initial value filtering.

    eq | ne | gt | lt <value> | between <lo> <hi>
        Start a new search when <space> is given, or narrow an active
        search when <space> is omitted.

    changed | unchanged | increased | decreased | incby <n> | decby <n>
        Narrow candidates relative to the previous scan snapshot.

    pointer <space> <target> | pointer rescan <target>
        Scan 4-byte aligned pointers P inside [base_start, base_end] where
        P <= target <= P + max_offset (default max_offset 0x1000), or
        rescan saved pointer bases against a moved target.

    undo
        Restore the previous candidate set and snapshot (up to 8 levels).

    list [<count>]
        Print up to <count> current candidates with live, previous, and
        delta values (default 20).

    reset
        Discard the active search session and undo history.

EXAMPLES
    search unknown WRAM -w 2 --dec
    search decby 5 --dec
    search undo
    search WRAM 64
    search list 10 --json
    search pointer RDRAM 80123450 --max-offset 400 --json

EXIT STATUS
    0   Search step completed (or usage/refusal message printed).
    1   No target attached, unknown space, invalid argument, or session
        service fault.

SEE ALSO
    memfind(1), snapshot(1), diff(1), cheat(1), mem(1), spaces(1)
